-- title:
--   Gluing rooted tests along shared root labels, with the product formula for rooted homomorphism counts
-- statement:
--   This module constructs the gluing of a family of rooted tests along a common set of root labels. Recall that a rooted test $F$ with $k$ roots and width parameter $t$ consists of a finite vertex set $X_F$, a symmetric relation $R_F$ on $X_F$ (loops are allowed), a root labelling $\rho_F:\{0,\dots,k-1\}\to X_F$ that need not be injective, and a rooted tree decomposition of $(X_F,R_F)$ whose bags have at most $t$ elements, in which every child bag meets its parent bag in fewer than $t$ elements, and whose root bag is exactly the set of roots. For a simple graph $G$ on $V$ and $x\in V^k$, a map of $F$ over $x$ is a map $f:X_F\to V$ with $f(\rho_F(j))=x_j$ for all $j$ and $f(p)f(q)\in E(G)$ whenever $p\,R_F\,q$; $\hom_x(F,G)$ denotes the number of such maps.
--
--   Fix an index set $I$, rooted tests $F_i$ with $r_i$ roots and a common width parameter $t$ ($i\in I$), and label maps $\eta_i:\{0,\dots,r_i-1\}\to\{0,\dots,m-1\}$ sending the root labels of $F_i$ to $m$ global labels.
--
--   1. **Root classes.** Two global labels $a,b$ are related if, for some $i$, there are root labels $j,l$ of $F_i$ with $\rho_{F_i}(j)=\rho_{F_i}(l)$, $a=\eta_i(j)$ and $b=\eta_i(l)$. The root classes are the classes of the equivalence relation generated by this relation; $[j]$ denotes the class of $j$.
--
--   2. **Glued vertices and gluing maps.** The glued vertex set $X$ is the disjoint union of the set of root classes and, for each $i$, the set of internal (non-root) vertices of $F_i$. The gluing map $\gamma_i:X_{F_i}\to X$ sends a root vertex $\rho_{F_i}(j)$ to $[\eta_i(j)]$ (this does not depend on the choice of $j$) and an internal vertex to its own copy.
--
--   3. **Glued relation.** $p\,R\,q$ holds iff $p=\gamma_i(u)$ and $q=\gamma_i(v)$ for some $i\in I$ and some $u\,R_{F_i}\,v$.
--
--   4. **Glued decomposition.** A new root node carries the bag of all root classes; every non-root node $n$ of the tree of $F_i$ becomes a node with bag $\gamma_i(B_n)$ and the same parent as in $F_i$, except that the children of the root of $F_i$ are attached to the new root. When $m\le t$ this is again a rooted tree decomposition of $(X,R)$ with bags of size at most $t$ and child–parent intersections of size less than $t$.
--
--   5. **Glued test.** For finite $I$ and $m\le t$, the glued test is the rooted test with $m$ roots $j\mapsto[j]$ on these data.
--
--   6. **Product formula.** For a graph $G$ on $V$ and $x\in V^m$, maps of the glued test over $x$ correspond bijectively to families $(f_i)_{i\in I}$ in which each $f_i$ is a map of $F_i$ over $x\circ\eta_i$: a glued map is restricted along each $\gamma_i$, and a family is reassembled by evaluating $x$ on the root classes. Consequently, for finite $V$,
--   $$
--   \hom_x(\text{glued test},G)=\prod_{i\in I}\hom_{x\circ\eta_i}(F_i,G).
--   $$
--
--   Gluing realizes a product of rooted homomorphism counts as the count of a single rooted test with the same width parameter. In the parity-lift argument it shows that the linear span of rooted test counts is closed under pointwise multiplication (glue two tests with $k$ roots along all their roots), which makes that span an algebra.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The root classes form `GlueRoot F η`, the Lean quotient `Quot (GlueRootRel F η)` (a quotient by the generated equivalence relation); the glued vertex type is `GlueVertex F η = GlueRoot F η ⊕ Σ i, GlueInternal F i`. The gluing map is `glueMap`, the decomposition `glueBagTree F η hm` (with highest bags `glueTop`), the test `glueTest F η hm`, the bijection `glueMapsEquiv` and the product formula `glueTest_count`, with counts valued in $\mathbb{Q}$. Gluing may create loops, for example when two related roots of some $F_i$ receive the same global label; a loop has no image in a simple graph, so such a test has count $0$. The private `dite_eq_left`/`dite_eq_right` lemmas are compatibility aliases.
